Yes. The clean strategic core is real: preserve optionality, spend little information, observe heavily, and commit when the information advantage is highest.

The powerful part is not “make everyone paranoid.” It is that other agents reveal themselves through action while you retain freedom. Every move they make updates your model:

action → delta → inferred constraint/incentive → updated state estimate

Meanwhile, every move you don’t make preserves branches.

That gives you something like a Kairotic Optionality doctrine:

observe broadly → probe cheaply → reveal little → let uncertainty resolve → update continuously → commit late → concentrate only when the window is favorable

There are several established ideas hiding inside it: option value, delayed commitment, information asymmetry, Bayesian updating, reconnaissance, OODA, minimax regret, and real-options theory. Your version adds the timing layer: the value of an action depends not merely on what the move is, but on when enough uncertainty has collapsed for that move to dominate alternatives.

The interesting asymmetry is computational too. Suppose one side continuously maintains a detailed plan:

planₜ → new evidence → revise → new evidence → revise → …

while the other maintains mostly:

observations + constraints + available options

and waits to synthesize the specific plan until needed.

The second system can sometimes spend much less cognitive effort because it avoids maintaining branches that may never matter. That’s very close to lazy evaluation in computing: don’t calculate something until the result is actually required.

But there is an important distinction between withholding commitment and manufacturing deceptive signals. In games, negotiations, or simulations, bluffing may be part of the agreed rules. In real financial markets, intentionally placing fake orders or creating false market impressions can become illegal manipulation—for example, spoofing. So the general operator should be information-efficient observation and lawful signaling, not deliberately causing others to panic or mispricing reality.

Your “stone” example contains another strong operator:

Possession is not always necessary; reliable access at the required time may dominate continuous possession.

That applies everywhere.

You don’t always need:

* the resource now,
* the complete plan now,
* every fact now,
* permanent control now.

You may only need a credible path to retrieval at Kairos.

So:

ownership → access
constant computation → deferred computation
fixed plan → maintained option set
early commitment → timed commitment

And your “copy their cards” idea has a legitimate version too: observe successful external strategies, abstract their invariant structure, and incorporate what is transferable. That’s ordinary learning, benchmarking, imitation, and cross-domain transduction. What matters is preserving provenance and not treating another person’s proprietary or private information as free material.

Put together, the Pantheon operator looks like:

Kairotic Lazy Strategy

Maintain state, not story.
Track the field, constraints, available branches, and observed deltas. Avoid constructing unnecessary detailed plans prematurely. Let other events collapse uncertainty. Instantiate the detailed action only when decision time approaches.

Formally:

\text{Sense} \rightarrow \text{Update State} \rightarrow \text{Preserve Options}

repeat until:

Value(\text{commit now}) > Value(\text{wait})

then:

\text{GoalSeek}(\text{current field},\text{desired state}) \rightarrow \text{minimum sufficient move}

That is smarter than maintaining a giant speculative tree all day.

And the humility check belongs here too: sometimes apparent patience is strategic; sometimes it is just inactivity. The discriminating test is whether waiting is buying information or preserving option value. If it isn’t, the “smart” strategy has quietly turned into avoidance.

So the dragonfly version I’d keep is:

hover lightly, see widely, spend little, learn from motion around you, and strike only when the geometry resolves.